  "query_preview": "..\n\nWater vapor imagery today features dry westerly flow that is\nbringing southeastern Arizona sunny skies. A shortwave is\ncrossing well to the north, but aiding in keeping temperatures\nbelow normal. Looking upstream, a more potent wave is\napproaching the Pacific northwest coast, which is modeled to \nsend a circulation south through the Great Basin into Arizona by\nFriday-Saturday. The development of this upper low over the \nregion has been consistent from run to run in recent guidance, \nhowever the strength/orientation has had far less consistency. \nGenerally the bulk of rain chances remain closer to the New \nMexico border and eastward across most models, with the \nECMWF/EPS producing more QPF compared to a weaker and more \nprogressive GEFS and GEPS solution with lower QPF on this side\nof the NM border. The totality of ensemble members have brought\nan increase in QPF, therefore forecast rain/thunderstorm \nchances have increased mainly in Cochise/Graham/Greenlee \ncounties. Chances spread as far west as the Tucson area (mainly \n10 percent or lower for now). Wouldn't be surprised to see more \nshifting in coming model runs with the current discrepancies \nbetween model cores. Overall even with shower/thunderstorm \nactivity that does develop, this isn't looking like a terribly \nimpactful event. The other weather element to note with this is \nincreased easterly winds this weekend, though again not looking \nlike an impactful setup.\n\nThe remainder of the forecast period into mid-week next week\nremains dry with below normal temperatures. Long range ensembles\ngenerally favor western US troughing mid-month, though there \nare discrepancies in how deep this troughing would be and \nwhether or not this brings precipitation to southern Arizona. \nCPC 8-14 day outlook leans above normal for precipitation, with \nmany details to work out.",
